[[eco-solving]]
=== Environment solving

[abstract]
A program's foreign packages must be installed, at compatible versions,
before its pools can compile or run. The compiler knows what a program
needs; the environment manager knows how to provide it. This section
specifies the contract between them: when the compiler asks, what it sends,
how the manager turns every program's requests into one solved set of
packages, and what holds when solves fail or run at once.

==== The build hook

`morloc make` asks for its packages only inside a managed environment,
when `MORLOC_ENV` is set (<<eco-environments>>). After writing the pool
sources and before compiling them, it runs:

----
$MORLOC_BUILD_HOOK sync --name <key> --spec envspec.json --root <project> [--installed]
----

`<key>` is the program's key (<<build-artifacts>>), `envspec.json` is the
document described in <<eco-modules>>, and `--installed` marks a build that
is being installed rather than built in place. The hook writes its messages
to standard error. Exit status 0 means the environment now satisfies the
specification; anything else fails the build. If `MORLOC_ENV` is set but no
hook is named, a program that declares foreign packages fails to build,
since nothing could provide them.

The hook asks the compiler named by `MORLOC_BIN` for its *language-support
table*: for each language, the runtime package and version range the
compiler supports and the packages its bindings need, plus the toolchain
the compiler itself needs. A program's request is always solved together
with this table, so a program can never ask for an interpreter the runtime
cannot bind.

Both documents carry versions. The dependency specification has an integer
version that the reader must match exactly; it is refused with "rebuild the
program" if older and "upgrade the manager" if newer. The language-support
table has a `MAJOR.MINOR` schema; a reader accepts any minor version of the
major it knows and ignores fields it does not recognise.

==== One world per environment

An environment has one set of installed packages, its *solved world*. It is
the solution of the union of:

 - the specification of every installed program;
 - the specification of every program built in place (*scratch* builds),
   kept until the environment is cleaned;
 - the compiler's language-support requirements;
 - extra packages the user named for the environment;
 - the interpreter pins (below).

One world means one version of each package. Two programs constraining the
same package must both be satisfied. A requirement nobody can satisfy fails
the command that added it.

Each requested package is provided, delegated or refused. conda and PyPI
packages are solved into the world. Rust crates and Julia packages are left
to the language's own build tool, which resolves them when the pool is
compiled. A source the manager does not support is refused, never silently
dropped, as are contradictory requests for one package: two conda channels,
two local paths, or a local path and a version.

A local Python package of an installed program (<<local-deps>>) is copied
into the environment under a name derived from a hash of its contents, and
the requirement points at the copy. The installed program then no longer
depends on the project directory, the same path is valid on the host, in a
container and in a frozen image, and reinstalling unchanged source does no
work. Copies that no installed program references are reclaimed.

==== Solving

Each environment has one solve lock, shared by the host and its container.
Recording a program's specification, gathering the world, solving it, and
recording the result all happen while holding it, so concurrent builds in
one environment serialize, and the second usually finds its world already
solved.

The world reduces to a canonical *requirement digest*. If it equals the
digest of the last successful solve and the solved packages are present,
nothing is done. The digest is written last, after the packages are
installed and their activation captured, so an interrupted solve is redone
rather than trusted.

Otherwise the solver first tries to install the existing lock unchanged,
and re-solves only if the lock cannot satisfy the new world. Package
versions therefore move only when a new requirement forces them to.

**Interpreter pins.** The Python and R bindings are compiled against one
minor version of their interpreter (<<eco-bootstrap>>). After the first
solve that provides an interpreter, its minor version is pinned, and later
solves stay within it. A requirement that needs another minor fails with a
message to update the environment, which drops the pin and rebuilds the
bindings.

**Failure.** If a solve fails, the requirements recorded for every program
are restored to what they were before the command. The solver's own
manifest, lock and partially installed packages may be left changed; the
next successful solve reconciles them, and the digest, removed at the start
of the solve, guarantees that it runs.

**Immutable environments.** An environment marked immutable -- one being
served, or a frozen image -- refuses any request that would change its
world or rebuild a binding, before writing anything.

==== When solving happens

[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Event | Solve
| `morloc make` in an environment | Through the build hook, as above.
| `mim install` | Once before the build, from the specification the compiler reports without building, so packages are present when the pools compile; then through the hook.
| `mim modify` (languages, extra packages) and `mim update` | A solve of the whole world, followed by a rebuild of the runtime and bindings when the Morloc version or an interpreter changed.
| Cleaning the environment | Scratch specifications are discarded and the world re-solved from installed programs only.
|===

